# Line-ending normalisation.
#
# Why: the team works across Windows and Linux. Without this, files copied or
# checked out on Windows pick up CRLF endings, and Git — which compares files
# byte-for-byte — reports every single line as modified. A one-line edit then
# shows up as a whole-file rewrite in the diff, which makes review impossible.
#
# `text=auto` tells Git to detect which files are text (as opposed to binary),
# and `eol=lf` tells it to store *and* check out those files with LF endings on
# every platform. Modern Windows editors handle LF fine, so there is no reason
# to keep CRLF in the working tree either.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly binary: never touch these bytes, never try to diff them as text.
# .npy files stay local per CLAUDE.md, but the rule guards against an accidental
# commit corrupting one through line-ending translation.
*.npy binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary

# Notebooks are JSON (text), so they normalise like any other text file, but
# marking them keeps the intent explicit for anyone reading this file.
*.ipynb text eol=lf
